> ## Documentation Index
> Fetch the complete documentation index at: https://docs.archil.com/llms.txt
> Use this file to discover all available pages before exploring further.

# TypeScript SDK

> Programmatic access to Archil disks from TypeScript and Node.js applications

The Archil TypeScript SDK is the [`disk`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/disk) package on npm. It's a pure-JavaScript control-plane client and CLI: create disks, list and inspect them, manage who can mount them, and run commands against them with [`disk.exec`](https://docs.archil.com/compute/serverless-execution).

```bash theme={null}
npm install disk
```

> [!NOTE]
>
> The npm package is named `disk`. The `archil` package on npm is an alias that installs and re-exports `disk`.

`disk` has no native dependencies and requires Node.js 22.19 or later. For the rare case where you need raw protocol access from Node (inodes, delegations, byte-level reads), there's a separate [`@archildata/native`](#low-level-protocol-access) package — most users will not need it.

| Package | When to use |
| - | - |
| [`disk`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/disk) | Manage disks, run commands with `disk.exec`, manage API keys. The default. |
| [`@archildata/native`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/@archildata/native) | Speak the Archil filesystem protocol directly from Node. Linux/macOS only. Not recommended unless you're building a custom client runtime. |

> [!NOTE]
>
> Looking for the FUSE mount client? That's the [`archil` CLI](https://docs.archil.com/mounting/linux), which mounts a disk as a real local filesystem. The TypeScript SDK does not mount disks; it talks to the control plane and runs serverless commands.

## Configuration

The recommended pattern is a module-namespace import with a one-time `configure` call:

```typescript theme={null}
import * as archil from "disk";

archil.configure({
  apiKey: process.env.ARCHIL_API_KEY,
  region: "aws-us-east-1",
});
```

Both options fall back to environment variables (`ARCHIL_API_KEY`, `ARCHIL_REGION`) if omitted, so in most environments `configure({})` — or skipping `configure` entirely — is enough.

For multi-tenant scripts that need multiple credentials in one process, instantiate `Archil` directly instead:

```typescript theme={null}
import { Archil } from "disk";

const prod = new Archil({ apiKey: prodKey, region: "aws-us-east-1" });
const staging = new Archil({ apiKey: stagingKey, region: "aws-us-east-1" });
```

For endpoints signed by a private certificate authority, pass `tls.ca` (a PEM string, `Buffer`, or array of either) to
`new Archil({ ..., tls: { ca } })`. Like Node's TLS `ca` option, it replaces the default trust list, so include
`getCACertificates()` from `node:tls` if you still need the public CAs.

> [!NOTE]
>
> The API key is an account-level credential and is **not** the same thing as a disk token. API keys authenticate calls to the control plane (everything in this page); a disk token grants mount access to a single disk. See the [disk users concept page](https://docs.archil.com/concepts/authorization.md#token-based-disk-authorization).

### Regions

`region` (or `ARCHIL_REGION`) must be one of these Archil region identifiers:

| Region | `region` value |
| - | - |
| AWS US East (N. Virginia) | `aws-us-east-1` |
| AWS US West (Oregon) | `aws-us-west-2` |
| AWS EU West (Ireland) | `aws-eu-west-1` |
| GCP US Central (Iowa) | `gcp-us-central1` |

You must use one of these identifiers and not a region for an individual cloud like `us-east-1`.

## Managing disks

```typescript theme={null}
import * as archil from "disk";

// Create a disk. It uses Archil-managed storage by default. The returned token
// is the disk token — save it; it isn't retrievable again.
const { disk, token } = await archil.createDisk({ name: "my-disk" });

// Optional: sync the disk with your own bucket (BYOB) instead
const { disk: byobDisk } = await archil.createDisk({
  name: "my-byob-disk",
  mounts: [{
    type: "s3",
    bucketName: "my-bucket",
    accessKeyId: "AKIA...",
    secretAccessKey: "...",
  }],
});

// List and look up disks
const all = await archil.listDisks();
const d = await archil.getDisk(disk.id);
```

Per-disk operations are methods on the `Disk` object itself, not top-level functions:

```typescript theme={null}
const d = await archil.getDisk("dsk-abc123");

// Add an additional mount token
const user = await d.createToken("ci");
// save user.token — it's only returned once

// Revoke access
await d.removeUser("token", user.identifier);

// Create keys for the S3 API — keep them server-side; the secret is only returned once
const s3 = await d.createS3Credentials({ nickname: "uploads", ttl: "24h" });
await d.removeS3Credentials(s3.s3AccessKeyId);

// Delete the disk (this does not delete data in your bucket)
await d.delete();
```

## Executing commands

`Disk.exec(command)` runs a bash command inside a Firecracker microVM with the file system already mounted, and returns stdout, stderr, exit code, and timing. See the [Serverless Execution concept page](https://docs.archil.com/compute/serverless-execution.md) for the full picture.

```typescript theme={null}
const d = await archil.getDisk("dsk-abc123");

const { stdout, stderr, exitCode, timing } = await d.exec(
  "du -sh logs",
);

console.log(`ran in ${timing.executeMs}ms (queued ${timing.queueMs}ms)`);
```

The disk is the working directory inside the microVM, so commands can reference files using relative paths.

Pass options as the second argument to choose the microVM's image, size, and network policy: `baseImage` or `imageId`, `vcpuCount`, `memSizeMib`, and `network`. These need `disk` 1.12.0 or later. See [Choosing an image](https://docs.archil.com/compute/serverless-execution.md#choosing-an-image) and [Choosing a size](https://docs.archil.com/compute/serverless-execution.md#choosing-a-size).

```typescript theme={null}
await d.exec("python3 train.py", {
  baseImage: "python:3.13",
  vcpuCount: 4,
  memSizeMib: 8192,
});
```

For multi-disk execs (mount several disks at once, optionally pinned to a subdirectory or read-only), call `archil.exec({ disks, command })` instead of `Disk.exec`; it takes the same options. See [Mounting multiple disks in one command](https://docs.archil.com/compute/serverless-execution.md#composing-multiple-disks-into-a-single-command).

### `ExecResult`

```typescript theme={null}
interface ExecResult {
  exitCode: number;
  stdout: string;
  stderr: string;
  timing: ExecTiming;
}

interface ExecTiming {
  /** End-to-end wall-clock measured on the server. */
  totalMs: number;
  /** Time spent queueing, scheduling, booting a microVM, and mounting the filesystem. */
  queueMs: number;
  /** Time the user's command itself ran, measured by the runtime. */
  executeMs: number;
}
```

Each invocation counts as one serverless tool call, with execution included. `executeMs` reports runtime,
not billable usage. See [Pricing & Billing](https://docs.archil.com/administration/billing.md#serverless-tool-calls).

The HTTP response returns after 5 minutes, and the response carries the first 4 MiB of stdout and the first 4 MiB of stderr. Write larger outputs to a file on the disk instead. See [Limits](https://docs.archil.com/compute/serverless-execution.md#limits).

### Fan-out

Because each `exec` runs in its own microVM, `Promise.all` is a map-reduce:

```typescript theme={null}
const files = (await d.exec("ls logs")).stdout.trim().split("\n");

const results = await Promise.all(
  files.map((f) => d.exec(`wc -l logs/${f}`)),
);
```

For the common case of *searching* files for a pattern, don't hand-roll this — use [`Disk.grep`](#searching-files), which fans the same work out for you and returns structured matches. See the [quickstart](https://docs.archil.com/getting-started/quickstart.md) for an end-to-end example wiring `disk.exec` into an AI agent loop.

## Searching files

`Disk.grep(opts)` searches the files on a disk for lines matching a regular expression. It fans the listing and matching out across many ephemeral microVMs so the search scales across many machines instead of one. It's the productized version of the fan-out above. Reach for it instead of `Promise.all` over `exec("grep …")` whenever you just want matching lines. See [Search Files](https://docs.archil.com/compute/search-files.md) for the full model.

```typescript theme={null}
const d = await archil.getDisk("dsk-abc123");

const { matches, stoppedReason, durationMs } = await d.grep({
  directory: "logs",
  pattern: "ERROR|FATAL",
  recursive: true,
});

for (const m of matches) {
  console.log(`${m.file}:${m.line}: ${m.text}`);
}
console.log(`${matches.length} matches in ${durationMs}ms (${stoppedReason})`);
```

You control search latency and scope with three knobs:

* `maxDurationSeconds` — wall-clock deadline (default 30, capped at 30).
* `concurrency` — max parallel workers (default 50). More workers can scan a large dataset faster; the request still counts as one call.
* `maxResults` — short-circuit once this many matches are collected (default 1000).

These options follow ripgrep's flags (`disk` 1.10.0 or later). See [Search files](https://docs.archil.com/compute/search-files.md#filtering-files) for examples:

* `ignoreCase` — match regardless of case (`rg -i`).
* `multiline` — let a match span lines (`rg -U`); such a match sets `endLine`.
* `globs` — include paths matching ripgrep `-g` globs relative to `directory`, or exclude them with a leading `!`.
* `types` / `typesNot` — search only, or skip, ripgrep file types such as `py` or `ts`.
* `contextBefore` / `contextAfter` — 0 to 10 lines of context around each match (`rg -B` / `rg -A`); the result then includes `groups`.

Always check `stoppedReason` — it tells you whether the search was exhaustive. When it stops early, the returned `matches` are a sample of whichever workers reported first, **not** the lexicographically first N:

| `stoppedReason` | Meaning |
| - | - |
| `completed` | Every file under the directory was scanned successfully. The matches are exhaustive. |
| `max_results` | Stopped after collecting `maxResults` matches, or 32 MiB of match and context text, before scanning everything. |
| `deadline` | Hit `maxDurationSeconds` before scanning everything. |
| `incomplete` | The pipeline finished but one or more batches errored (invalid regex, unreadable file) or exceeded a worker's 4 MiB output limit. Results may be partial. |
| `list_failed` | Directory listing failed; only partial results, if any, are present. |

### `GrepResult`

```typescript theme={null}
interface GrepResult {
  matches: GrepMatch[];
  groups?: GrepGroup[];             // only with contextBefore or contextAfter
  stoppedReason: GrepStoppedReason; // "completed" | "incomplete" | "max_results" | "deadline" | "list_failed"
  filesScanned: number;
  containersDispatched: number;
  computeSecondsUsed: number; // summed worker runtime; not a billing unit
  durationMs: number;         // end-to-end wall clock
  listingMs: number;          // overlaps grepMs, so the two can sum past durationMs
  grepMs: number;
}

interface GrepMatch {
  file: string;     // relative to the disk root
  line: number;     // 1-based; a multiline match's first line
  endLine?: number; // a multiline match's last line
  text: string;     // the matching line, or every line a multiline match covers
}

interface GrepGroup {
  file: string;
  lines: { line: number; text: string; match: boolean }[]; // match: false for context lines
}
```

Each `grep` request counts as one serverless tool call, regardless of worker count or runtime. See [Pricing & Billing](https://docs.archil.com/administration/billing.md#serverless-tool-calls).

## Persistent sandboxes

`Archil.sandboxes` manages persistent Linux VMs. Each sandbox has a dedicated disk, accepts multiple
commands over its lifetime, and can preserve CPU and memory state across pause and resume. See
[Persistent sandboxes](https://docs.archil.com/compute/sandboxes/introduction.md) for the lifecycle, interactive shells,
services, forks, and limits.

```typescript theme={null}
import { Archil } from "disk";

const client = new Archil({
  apiKey: process.env.ARCHIL_API_KEY,
  region: "aws-us-east-1",
});

const sandbox = await client.sandboxes.create({
  name: "prepared-environment",
  baseImage: "node:24-bookworm",
  vcpuCount: 4,
  memSizeMiB: 8192,
  env: { NODE_ENV: "development" },
  maxTtlSeconds: 14_400,
  maxConcurrentExecs: 32,
});

const execution = await sandbox.run("node --version");
const result = await execution.wait();
console.log(result.stdout);

await sandbox.pause();
await sandbox.resume();
await sandbox.stop();
await sandbox.start();

const fork = await sandbox.fork({ name: "agent-task" });
await fork.stop();
await fork.delete();
```

`maxTtlSeconds` sets the hard timeout from 60 seconds to 24 hours (the default). `idleTtlSeconds` sets
the idle timeout from 0 to 24 hours. It defaults to 30 minutes, and 0 disables it. Both pause the VM, preserving memory and
processes for `resume()`. Use `sandbox.setTimeout()` to update either setting; a session cannot exceed 24 hours
even after a timeout reset. See
[Setting a time to live](https://docs.archil.com/compute/sandboxes/introduction.md#setting-a-time-to-live) for examples and reset behavior.

`create`, `pause`, `resume`, `stop`, `start`, and `fork` wait for completion by default. The
server handles the initial wait; if its request budget expires first, the SDK continues polling.
Pass `{ wait: false }` to return as soon as the operation is accepted. A waited `pause()` throws
`SandboxPauseError` if the sandbox does not reach `paused`. `fork()` pauses a running sandbox while
the snapshot is taken and resumes it once the fork is accepted.

```typescript theme={null}
const running = await sandbox.run("sleep 600", {
  timeoutSeconds: 600,
});

const processId = running.id;
const cursor = running.cursor;
await running.disconnect();

const resumed = await sandbox.attach(processId, { offset: cursor });
await resumed.kill();
```

Use `client.sandboxes.list()` and `.get(id)` to retrieve sandboxes. A `Sandbox` provides
`refresh`, `fork`, `delete`, `pause`, `resume`, `stop`, `start`, and `setTimeout`; `exposePort`, `listPorts`,
`unexposePort`, and port-token methods for [network services](https://docs.archil.com/compute/sandboxes/introduction.md#hosting-network-services);
`getNetwork` and `updateNetwork` for the [firewall](https://docs.archil.com/compute/sandboxes/firewall.md); plus `run` and `attach` for starting and reattaching to sandbox processes.
`exec` runs a command and waits for its result. Pass an absolute `cwd` to `run` or `exec` to choose a working directory.
The older `processes.start` and `processes.connect` methods are deprecated compatibility aliases.

For an interactive command, provide terminal dimensions. The returned `SandboxProcess` accepts
input and terminal resizes while streaming output to `onOutput`:

```typescript theme={null}
const shell = await sandbox.run("/bin/sh", {
  terminal: { cols: 120, rows: 40 },
  onOutput: ({ data }) => process.stdout.write(data),
});

await shell.sendInput("pwd\n");
await shell.resize({ cols: 160, rows: 50 });
const { exitCode } = await shell.wait();
```

### Mounting existing disks

Pass `mounts` when creating a sandbox to attach existing disks in addition to its internal root disk:

```typescript theme={null}
const workspace = await client.sandboxes.create({
  mounts: [{ disk: "dsk-0123456789abcdef", path: "/mnt/archil" }],
});
console.log((await workspace.exec("ls /mnt/archil")).stdout);

await workspace.stop();
await workspace.start({ mounts: [] });
```

Each mount accepts `disk` (a disk ID or `Disk` object), `path`, `subdirectory`, `readOnly`, `conditional`, and `queueMs`.
With a single mount, `path` is optional and defaults to `/mnt/archil`.
Omitting `mounts` on `start()` keeps saved mounts; `[]` clears them and a list replaces them. See
[Persistent sandboxes](https://docs.archil.com/compute/sandboxes/introduction.md#attaching-an-archil-disk) for sharing and forking behavior.

### Private images

```typescript theme={null}
const image = await client.images.build({
  source: "ghcr.io/acme/agent-runtime:v2",
  registryAuth: { username: "bot", password: REDACTED },
});
const agent = await client.sandboxes.create({ imageId: image.imageId });
```

`images.build()` waits until the image is ready, retrying interrupted builds up to three times. The default timeout
is 30 minutes; set it with `images.build(request, { timeoutSeconds: 600 })`. Failed builds raise `ImageBuildError`;
timeouts raise `ArchilError` with code `IMAGE_BUILD_TIMEOUT`. Use `images.get(imageId)` to read the current build state.

Pass `imageId` instead of `baseImage` when creating a sandbox from a built image.
See [Sandbox images](https://docs.archil.com/compute/sandboxes/images.md) for reusing builds and updating tags.

## Reading and writing objects

A `Disk` doubles as an S3-compatible bucket: read, write, delete, and list its files by key without mounting it. These methods talk to Archil's S3 endpoint using your same API key, with no separate S3 credentials or SigV4 signing on your part.

```typescript theme={null}
const d = await archil.getDisk("dsk-abc123");
const report = { generated: "2026-01", rows: 1234 };

// Write — body is a string, Uint8Array/Buffer, or ArrayBuffer. contentType is
// optional (defaults to application/octet-stream). Returns the etag.
const { etag } = await d.putObject(
  "reports/2026-01/data.json",
  JSON.stringify(report),
  "application/json",
);

// Read — returns a Uint8Array.
const bytes = await d.getObject("reports/2026-01/data.json");
const text = new TextDecoder().decode(bytes);

// Metadata / existence without downloading the body
const meta = await d.headObject("reports/2026-01/data.json"); // null if absent
if (await d.objectExists("reports/2026-01/data.json")) {
  // ...
}

// Delete (idempotent — deleting a missing key resolves successfully)
await d.deleteObject("reports/2026-01/data.json");
```

`putObject` handles any size with one call. Small bodies go through a single request, and larger bodies are uploaded as a multipart upload automatically: split into parts, uploaded with bounded concurrency, and assembled. If any part fails, the upload is aborted so nothing is left half-staged. You don't pick a different method for big files. Tune the switch point and parallelism with options:

```typescript theme={null}
const { etag } = await d.putObject("backups/2026-01.tar", bigBytes, {
  contentType: "application/x-tar",
  multipartThreshold: 5 * 1024 * 1024, // switch to multipart above 5 MiB; default = partSize
  partSize: 32 * 1024 * 1024,          // ≥ 5 MiB; default 16 MiB
  concurrency: 8,                      // parts in flight at once; default 4
});
```

For very large objects the part size grows automatically so the upload never exceeds S3's 10,000-part limit.

`listObjects` auto-paginates by default, returning every matching key. The first argument is a key prefix; a non-recursive listing (the default) returns the immediate level as `objects` plus subdirectory `commonPrefixes`:

```typescript theme={null}
const oneLevel = await d.listObjects("reports/");                  // one level
const subtree = await d.listObjects("reports/", { recursive: true }); // whole subtree
const first100 = await d.listObjects("reports/", { limit: 100 });   // cap the total

// Stream pages instead of buffering everything (large listings):
for await (const page of d.listObjectsPages("reports/")) {
  for (const obj of page.objects) {
    console.log(obj.key, obj.size, obj.lastModified);
  }
}

// Or drive pagination yourself:
const page = await d.listObjects("reports/", { singlePage: true });
if (page.isTruncated) {
  const next = await d.listObjects("reports/", {
    singlePage: true,
    continuationToken: page.nextContinuationToken,
  });
}
```

`deleteObjects` removes many keys in one round trip (auto-batched at S3's 1,000-key limit). Unlike `deleteObject`, per-key failures are returned rather than thrown:

```typescript theme={null}
const { deleted, errors } = await d.deleteObjects(["a.txt", "logs/b.txt", "c.txt"]);
for (const e of errors) console.warn(`${e.key}: ${e.code} ${e.message}`);
```

`appendObject` appends bytes to an existing object (creating it if absent) — handy for log-style writes. Each call may append at most 1 MiB; append in chunks to grow past that:

```typescript theme={null}
await d.appendObject("logs/app.log", "first line\n");
await d.appendObject("logs/app.log", "second line\n"); // concatenated
```

Objects are created as `root:root` with mode `0644` by default. To let a non-root user on a mount edit them, such as an agent sandbox running as uid `1000`, pass `mode`, `uid`, and `gid`:

```typescript theme={null}
await d.putObject("workspace/notes.md", text, { mode: 0o640, uid: 1000, gid: 1000 });
```

Parent directories the write creates get the same `uid` and `gid` with mode `0755`. `appendObject` and `multipart.create` take the same options; `appendObject` applies them only when it creates the file. An overwrite replaces the owner and mode too, so pass them on every write. See [Ownership and permissions](https://docs.archil.com/protocols/s3-api.md#ownership-and-permissions) for the full rules.

For manual control over the multipart lifecycle (e.g. uploading parts from separate processes), the raw S3 primitives live in the opt-in `d.multipart` namespace — `create`, `uploadPart`, `complete`, `abort`, `listParts`, `listUploads`. Most code never needs these; prefer `putObject`, which runs the lifecycle for you.

```typescript theme={null}
const { uploadId } = await d.multipart.create("big.bin");
const p1 = await d.multipart.uploadPart("big.bin", uploadId, 1, firstChunk);
const p2 = await d.multipart.uploadPart("big.bin", uploadId, 2, secondChunk);
await d.multipart.complete("big.bin", uploadId, [p1, p2]);
```

Transient failures (HTTP 429 and 5xx, plus network errors) are retried automatically with jittered exponential backoff before surfacing; other 4xx are caller errors and aren't retried. The two non-idempotent operations (`completeMultipartUpload` and `appendObject`) are not auto-retried, since a retry after a succeeded-but-unacknowledged call would return a spurious `NoSuchUpload` or duplicate the appended bytes.

Object-API failures throw `ArchilS3Error` (a subclass of `ArchilError`) with `status` (HTTP status), `code` (the S3 error code, e.g. `"NoSuchKey"`), `requestId`, and the raw XML body on `raw`. `getObject` on a missing key throws a 404 — use `headObject` / `objectExists` to probe without catching.

```typescript theme={null}
import { ArchilS3Error } from "disk";

try {
  await d.getObject("reports/missing.json");
} catch (e) {
  if (e instanceof ArchilS3Error) {
    console.log(e.status, e.code, e.requestId);
  }
}
```

## Share URLs

`share` generates a signed, time-limited URL that lets anyone download a file from the disk without credentials:

```typescript theme={null}
const share = await d.share("reports/2026-08/summary.csv");
console.log(share.url);       // https://control.…/api/shared/<token>
console.log(share.expiresIn); // 86400 (seconds)

// Custom expiry — any value from 1 to 604800 (7 days)
const hourShare = await d.share("reports/2026-08/summary.csv", { expiresIn: 3600 });
```

The returned URL supports HTTP `Range` requests and streams with a sensible `Content-Type`. After expiry, requests to the URL return `401`.

## Managing API keys

API keys are account-level, so these helpers live at the top level rather than on a `Disk`:

```typescript theme={null}
await archil.listApiKeys();
const k = await archil.createApiKey({ name: "ci-bot", description: "GitHub Actions" });
// save k.token — it's only returned once
await archil.deleteApiKey("key-abc123");
```

## CLI

`disk` ships a CLI under the same name. See the [`disk` CLI reference](https://docs.archil.com/reference/disk-cli) for the full command list.

```bash theme={null}
npx disk create my-disk
npx disk list
npx disk dsk-abc123 exec "ls -la"
```

## Low-level protocol access

For the small number of integrations that need to speak Archil's filesystem protocol directly from Node — inodes, delegations, byte-level reads, paginated directory enumeration — install [`@archildata/native`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/@archildata/native) alongside `disk`:

```bash theme={null}
npm install disk @archildata/native
```

Then call `Disk.mount()`, which lazy-loads the native client:

```typescript theme={null}
import { getDisk } from "disk";

const d = await getDisk("dsk-abc123");
const client = await d.mount({ authToken: process.env.ARCHIL_DISK_TOKEN });

// `client` is an ArchilClient from @archildata/native — see that package's
// README on npm for the full inode-level API.
await client.close();
```

`@archildata/native` ships prebuilt binaries for **Linux** (x64, arm64, glibc) and **macOS** (arm64). On other platforms, `mount()` throws; the rest of `disk` still works.

> [!NOTE]
>
> If you're tempted to reach for `@archildata/native` to "run a bash command on a disk," use [`disk.exec`](#executing-commands) instead. It gives you a real shell with the filesystem mounted in a Firecracker microVM, with no native dependencies on the caller side.

## Need a bash executor inside Node?

The legacy [`@archildata/just-bash`](https://docs.archil.com/mounting/just-bash) package implements a JavaScript bash interpreter that talks to a disk through `@archildata/native`. It's still published, but for almost every use case `disk.exec` is the better answer. It runs your command in a real Firecracker microVM with the filesystem mounted, returns stdout/stderr/exit code, and has no native dependencies on the caller.


